{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"3662f6bf-3bd9-4495-adbd-363edeca31f3","title":"Area between two curves"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3662f6bf-3bd9-4495-adbd-363edeca31f3","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Area between curves comes from adding vertical strips. Let's compare the line y equals two x with the parabola y equals x squared.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Area between two curves"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.2,2.3],"y":[-0.3,4.8],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"line","y":"2*x","domain":[0,2.15],"label":"y = 2x"},{"kind":"fn","id":"parabola","y":"x^2","domain":[0,2.15],"label":"y = x²"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3662f6bf-3bd9-4495-adbd-363edeca31f3","step":{"id":"s2","script":"First find where the curves meet. Setting their heights equal gives x equals zero and x equals two: our integration limits.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"intersections","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"x^2=2x\\;\\Rightarrow\\;x(x-2)=0\\;\\Rightarrow\\;x=0,2"},{"op":"figure","id":"bounds","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"start","at":{"on":"line","x":0},"label":"(0, 0)"},{"kind":"point","id":"finish","at":{"on":"line","x":2},"label":"(2, 4)"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"finish"},"to":[2,0],"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3662f6bf-3bd9-4495-adbd-363edeca31f3","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Between these intersections, the line is above the parabola. Each strip's height is upper minus lower; integrate that height across the interval.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"area_strip","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"shade","between":["line","parabola"],"from":0,"to":2},{"kind":"point","id":"strip_low","at":{"on":"parabola","x":1}},{"kind":"point","id":"strip_high","at":{"on":"line","x":1}},{"kind":"segment","id":"strip","from":{"at":"strip_low"},"to":{"at":"strip_high"},"label":"2x − x²"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"integral","anchor":{"el":"intersections","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\int_0^2\\bigl(2x-x^2\\bigr)\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why do we subtract x² from 2x, rather than the other way around?","expected_gist":"The line is above the parabola on this interval."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3662f6bf-3bd9-4495-adbd-363edeca31f3","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Integrating upper minus lower gives four thirds square units. The shaded region represents that positive area, not the area beneath either curve alone.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"integral","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\left[x^2-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2=4-\\frac83=\\boxed{\\frac43\\text{ square units}}"},{"op":"figure","id":"area_label","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.48,2.65],"text":"A","size":"normal"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"3662f6bf-3bd9-4495-adbd-363edeca31f3","accepted_steps":4,"repairs":0,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
